WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
First of all, I'm not perfect. I'm

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.320
learning a lot. My oldest kid is only

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.040
seven. I talk to a lot of very wealthy

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.400
people who have awesome kids and I try

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.080
to get advice from them because I think

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.640
that's pretty rare when somebody has

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.920
significant wealth and has good kids

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:17.160
instead of, you know, kids.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.560
But like I am I am not putting a bubble

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.840
around my kids.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.240
I'm letting them fail when the stakes

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.720
are low.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.000
I'm not trying to protect them from all

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.320
pain.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.760
I'm not

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.960
catching them if their bike is starting

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.400
to tip over.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.599
I'm not stopping them if I know that

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.520
they left something out and the dog is

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.320
going to chew it up and they're going to

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.160
have to go without that toy. Like I'm

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.720
letting them make these low

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.200
um I'm letting them like not do their

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.560
homework and like get in trouble by the

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.920
teacher and not get their sticker and

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.160
not get to go on Friday to pick out a

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.600
toy like all the other kids and then

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.440
they come home crying.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.120
Like those are low

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.640
those are other other parents would just

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.160
stop that. They would put their kid in a

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.640
bubble and protect them, protect them,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.280
protect them.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.240
I'm like letting them getting used to

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.200
the fact that hey, if you make a

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.720
decision, you live with the

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.200
consequences.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.520
Maybe it's maybe they're 18 and they

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.440
drop an iPhone and they're like, "Dad, I

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.160
don't have enough money to get a new

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.640
iPhone." I'll be like, "Huh." [laughter]

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.480
Well,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.840
you know, it just keeps going up and

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:12.360
then when you're 25 and you make or

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.800
maybe if you start a little lower at 12,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.880
they drop the phone and then at 18,

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.080
they're at a party and it's 2:00 a.m.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.160
and they make the decision to drive

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.360
home.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.080
Like am I going to be there to bail them

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.560
out of jail? Like that's a that's a

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.640
Like the decisions as you go up in life

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.440
get more and more intense. So, I went to

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.840
Cornell where I watched my floor

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:35.040
10 of the kids there had their parents

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.280
do every single thing for them to get

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.520
them into Cornell.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.240
Watched them do their homework, wrote

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.560
their college essays, made every single

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.720
decision for them so that their kids

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.560
could not think for themselves. They

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.800
were robots.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.160
Those kids got to college and

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.560
they couldn't figure out how to get from

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.760
North Campus to College Town to go to a

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.480
party. They're calling their moms saying

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.760
like, "When's the bus schedule? Or

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.320
should I get a cab? It's a little bit

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.960
cold, should I walk?" It blew my mind at

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.200
how dependent these kids were on their

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:00.800
parents. Totally incompetent. I don't

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.280
know how they ever hacked it and made it

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.120
in life, honestly.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.880
So,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.600
I'm letting my kids build the muscle

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.039
decision-making. And the second part is

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.240
I'm trying to turn them into

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:12.080
communicators. Like they can look an

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.800
adult, my 7-year-old can look an adult

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.120
in their eye and ask them a question

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.080
about themselves.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.880
I'll I'll be with a buddy and I'll walk

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.480
in the Chick-fil-A and I'll say, "Hey

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.959
guys, just so you know, my 7-year-old's

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:23.360
going to come in here to order. I'm

00:02:21.959 --> 00:02:25.720
right outside. He's going to tell you

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.920
what he wants." Um

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:29.200
just hang with me. And they're like, "Oh

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.240
yeah, awesome." And I make him go in and

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.360
place the family's order.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.800
Little things like Little things like

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.240
that. That just a little bit of

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.560
uncomfort that I'm kind of getting them

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.160
used to

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.600
you know, used to doing.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.520
Yeah, I like that. I would I make my son

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.560
go in and pay for gas. Uh he uh he hates

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.840
it, you know. Even picking up Even

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.840
picking up the phone. Like my parents

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.040
used to make me call and set a

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.840
reservation for us to go to dinner.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.880
And like most high school kids now can't

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:55.440
pick up the phone and call somebody

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.560
because they have so much anxiety around

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.161
that. Mhm. That ain't going to fly in my

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:58.816
house.

00:02:58.161 --> 00:02:59.120
>> [snorts]

00:02:58.816 --> 00:03:00.200
[laughter]

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.080
>> I like that.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.160
Your your tweet uh and this is I think

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:07.720
it was a while ago, but your tweet about

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.480
your son dropping the ice cream. Like I

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.440
don't know. To me it was a perfect tweet

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.760
and and the hate you got over that was

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.280
insane. I embellished this I embellished

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.400
the story because I knew it was going to

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.120
fire people up. He ate nearly the whole

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.959
thing and he set it down and it fell

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.200
over. And then he started to cry because

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:23.959
he wanted another one. I'm like, "Uh

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.760
bud." Like so I told everybody he

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:28.000
dropped it. He saved up money all week

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.480
working a job, bought the ice cream,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.320
dropped it after the second lick, and I

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.120
refused to buy him another one.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.497
And yeah, they got they got very very

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.880
upset, but it was it wasn't fully true.

00:03:35.497 --> 00:03:38.480
>> [laughter]

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.737
>> I have a heart.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:41.360
Right.

00:03:40.737 --> 00:03:42.800
>> [laughter]

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.519
>> Uh that's great.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.480
All right. So So just a little bit ago

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:49.160
you brought up you brought up leverage.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.320
And and it's not always money that's

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.640
leverage in a business. You can you can

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.480
use systems, you can use teams. Yes, you

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.640
can use financing and other people's

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.760
money.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.320
What does good leverage look like for a

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:04.920
solopreneur

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.160
trying to maybe go the multi-business

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:09.040
route like like you have?

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.280
Well, leverage for them is to hire that

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.400
first international employee for $800 a

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.320
month and get 20 hours back. And then

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:19.120
instantly their hourly their time their

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.840
hourly wage goes from 25 to $40 an hour

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.920
when they make that move. And then they

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.600
can think outside their business. They

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.560
can work on things that are important,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.440
you know, but not urgent. They can

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.640
actually think about growing their

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.919
company instead of just being stuck in

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:31.840
the weeds blocking and tackling.

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:32.760
Um that's the smallest form of leverage

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.400
and the

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.000
end goal, the ultimate form of leverage

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.640
in my opinion, is just to not have to do

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.720
business with

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.800
Like I have a whole section in my book

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:44.919
about like, what is leverage? Leverage

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:47.680
is not is living in a world where one

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:50.760
person knows no one person has the

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.560
ability to mess up my situation.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:53.960
Almost nobody can say that. They have a

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.840
business partner that has a majority.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.640
They have a main investor. If they pull

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.080
the plug, something would be off. They

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.840
have Amazon.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:04.200
The their their manager at Amazon makes

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.000
one decision and it up everything

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.560
for them. And then there's like people

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.960
who work a job where if their boss

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.720
doesn't think they're doing a good job

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.760
and wants to let them go,

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.680
their entire ability to feed their

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:15.800
family is gone. They have no leverage.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.280
But if you can get to a point where you

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.120
have

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.680
enough investments,

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.600
enough assets, enough cash,

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.960
um enough of a network that you don't

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.880
have to do business with and

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.440
not a single one person could mess up

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.800
your world,

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.640
then then you have the ultimate form of

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.640
leverage in my opinion.

